----------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. THE FINDING
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
MoE expert deltas are CHEAPER in absolute terms than dense ones:
    3.54 (MXFP4)  <  4.20-4.31 (IQ3_XXS)  <  4.94 (IQ2_XS)  <  6.86-7.22 (dense)
That is real, and it is about HALF the dense cost at the coarsest expert base.

But the MoE spectrum is NEARLY FLAT -- sigma_1/sigma_R of 3.0, against 482-3146
for dense weights. The MoE expert delta is the LEAST structured thing measured
in this entire investigation. It is not diffuse in the way dense weights are
diffuse; it is closer to isotropic.

And that settles the question. Reaching 99% of its energy needs rank 247-248 of
256, at 38.6-38.8 bits/element -- roughly NINE TIMES worse than simply
order-0 coding the delta, and WORSE than the 32-bit original weights.

    Making the delta cheaper helped the ORDER-0 option and did nothing for the
    LOW-RANK option. A cheaper delta is not a structured delta.

Every available mechanism for compressing the correction field loses:

  SPARSE_DELTA     = REFUTED   2-13% near-zero
  LOW_RANK_DELTA   = REFUTED   rank 188-248 of 256 for 99% of energy, 0/21
  LOW_ENTROPY      = REFUTED   3.5-7.2 bits/elem, and that IS the floor
  FP16_FULL_DELTA  = SURVIVES  -- but at ~7 bits/elem it is not a compression win

=> RESIDUAL_OVERLAY_COMPRESSION = NOT VIABLE across the entire reachable
   population: dense Q4_K, dense Q6_K, and every routed-expert format
   (IQ2_XS, IQ3_XXS, MXFP4) of a real 104 GB MoE model.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. WHY THE i-QUANTS ARE NOW MEASURABLE AT ALL
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
They were previously declared unmeasurable, on the reasoning that 256-4096
codebook bytes cannot be verified if recalled: one wrong entry yields a
self-consistent decode that passes every stationarity test and yields a rank
curve indistinguishable from a real one.

That reasoning was CORRECT ABOUT RECALL and WRONG ABOUT AVAILABILITY. A
vendored ggml reference exists in-tree:

  .kilo\worktrees\equal-viscountess\3rdparty\ggml\src\ggml-common.h
  SHA256 01FC6F1E37F65E08E717FDBDC00713DC22A10BD3E69F49E72CCA2E4F8E04E58A

tools/iq_table_extract.ps1 PARSES the GGML_TABLE_BEGIN blocks and emits
certs/iq_tables.inc with count verification:

  kmask_iq2xs      8/8      iq2xs_grid    512/512
  ksigns_iq2xs   128/128    iq3xxs_grid   256/256
  TABLE_COUNT_VERIFICATION=PASS
  GENERATED_SHA256 = FD4F6507657A6DACD370482C0189B658B1E5D6707513D5E9355782D77BBE699B

NOTHING WAS TRANSCRIBED BY HAND. A 4096-byte hand copy is precisely the
operation that cannot be checked afterwards, so a script performs it instead
and the generated file carries its source path, line ranges and reference hash.

Block geometry was also taken from the reference static_asserts rather than
recalled:  block_iq2_xs  = 2 + QK_K/8*2 + QK_K/32 = 74 bytes
                        block_iq3_xxs = 2 + 3*QK_K/8    = 98 bytes


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. WHY THESE DECODES ARE TRUSTWORTHY
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
IQ_LATTICE_MATCH_FRACTION = 1.000000 on EVERY i-quant case.

By construction, each decoded group is exactly one entry of the codebook scaled
by db with a sign mask applied. So every group's |value| pattern is looked up in
the extracted table and must match EXACTLY ONE entry. A wrong port, a wrong
table, a wrong sign index, a wrong scale or a wrong block offset ALL break that
lookup.

A match fraction of exactly 1.000000 is therefore evidence that the port and
the generated table are simultaneously correct -- strictly stronger than
stationarity, which a wrong codebook can satisfy by accident.

MXFP4 has its own equivalent: post_norm_max lands on exactly 6.000000, the E2M1
top magnitude, and normalised row-RMS cv is 0.041-0.043.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. DEFECTS AND CORRECTIONS IN THIS PASS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
C1  TYPE MAP WRONG ON 3 OF 4, asserted from memory (section 1). Corrected by
    reading ggml.h. A receipt that named formats it had not verified could have
    sent the next author after the wrong codebooks entirely.

C2  BLOCK OVERRUN, INTRODUCED BY ME, CAUGHT BY THE INSTRUMENT. Making the
    window width a parameter did not widen the read loop, so `raw` was sized
    for R x blocksWide blocks but filled with R. post_norm_max came back 1.6e37
    against an E2M1 ceiling of 6.0 and the check refused it. That overrun
    produced entropy 3.539 / rank99 30 / cost 7.5 bits per element -- which
    read without the FAIL would have been the FIRST FAVOURABLE RESULT in this
    entire investigation.

C3  CRITERION MISMATCHES REPLACED, NOT RELAXED. The distinct-value floor (>=64,
    inherited from Q4_K where scales are per 256) fails every correct MXFP4
    decode, which is lattice-confined to 23. The raw stationarity bound
    (cv < 1.0) is wrong for MXFP4 because per-32 E8M0 scales make cv ~6
    inherent; after normalisation the criterion is STRICTER and measures 0.04.
    In both cases the gate was replaced by the property that actually
    discriminates, rather than loosened until it passed.

C4  AN INSTRUMENT THAT CANNOT DISAGREE IS NOT AN INSTRUMENT. Three separate
    times in this campaign a broken measurement produced a result favourable to
    the hypothesis under test (see C2). Every one was caught by a check, and
    every one had to be discarded despite being publishable.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. LIMITS THAT REMAIN
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Windows are 256x1024 (and 256x256 for the dense matrix). Per-tensor rather
    than per-window low-rank fits are not done, so "this tensor is low rank"
    and "this window is low rank" are still not separated.
  * Layer coverage is 0 / 20 / 41-43, not exhaustive across 43 layers.
  * ffn_up_exps of the MoE model is not sampled; only ffn_down_exps and
    ffn_gate_exps.
  * No higher-order (context) entropy. Order-0 is already within a factor of
    ~1.1 of the 8-bit symbol, so little headroom, but unmeasured.
  * One model per architecture family. DeepSeek-style MoE and a dense Qwen.
  * No engine, GPU, I/O or residency linkage anywhere in this chain.

  None of these limits is load-bearing for the verdict: the low-rank cost is
  9x worse than the competing order-0 baseline in every case, and the spectrum
  is flat in every case. A broader sample could change the numbers by a few
  percent; it would not move a 9x gap.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
